
To run CameraChessWeb on a Raspberry Pi with PiCam:

Prerequisites
Raspberry Pi 4 or 5 (recommended for performance)
PiCamera 2 or compatible camera module
Node.js 18+ installed on RPi
Camera enabled in raspi-config
Steps
Install dependencies on RPi

bash
sudo apt update
sudo apt install -y build-essential python3 git
curl -fsSL https://deb.nodesource.com/setup_18.x | sudo -E bash -
sudo apt install -y nodejs
Clone and setup

bash
git clone https://github.com/Pbatch/CameraChessWeb.git
cd CameraChessWeb
npm install
Build for RPi

bash
npm run build
Run development server

bash
npm run dev
Access from RPi browser: http://localhost:5173
Access from another device: http://<rpi-ip>:5173
Camera Access on RPi
The browser needs camera permissions. For headless/remote setups:

Use libcamera (modern RPi OS) instead of legacy raspistill
Enable camera in browser permissions
May need to run with --use-fake-device-for-media-stream for testing
Performance Considerations
RPi 4: Should handle inference but may be slower
RPi 5: Recommended for real-time piece detection
Consider adding GPU acceleration with TensorFlow.js WebGL backend
Monitor memory usage (RPi has limited RAM)
Issues & Troubleshooting
Check the project's Discord for RPi-specific setup help.

through thonny
To run CameraChessWeb on Raspberry Pi through Thonny:

Setup
Install Thonny on RPi

bash
sudo apt install thonny
Open Thonny and configure Python interpreter

Tools → Options → Interpreter
Select system Python or create virtual environment
Create a launcher script
In Thonny, create a new file run_chesscam.py:

Python
import subprocess
import webbrowser
import time
import os

# Navigate to project directory
os.chdir('/home/pi/CameraChessWeb')  # Adjust path as needed

# Install dependencies if needed
subprocess.run(['npm', 'install'], check=True)

# Start dev server
print("Starting CameraChessWeb dev server...")
server = subprocess.Popen(['npm', 'run', 'dev'])

# Wait for server to start
time.sleep(5)

# Open browser
print("Opening browser to http://localhost:5173")
webbrowser.open('http://localhost:5173')

try:
    # Keep server running
    server.wait()
except KeyboardInterrupt:
    print("\nShutting down...")
    server.terminate()
Run in Thonny
Click the Run button (or F5)
Browser will auto-open to the app
Alternative: Run terminal command from Thonny
Python
import subprocess
subprocess.run(['npm', 'run', 'dev'], cwd='/home/pi/CameraChessWeb')
Notes
Thonny is primarily for Python - this project is Node.js/TypeScript
You'll still need Node.js/npm installed on RPi
For camera access in browser, use the same URL/permission approach as before
Consider running npm run dev directly in terminal for better performance monitoring
Better approach: Use Thonny for Python helper scripts, run the app in terminal directly.
